==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|δενδρίτης||of or pertaining to a tree}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈdɛndɹaɪt/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|cytology}} A slender projection of a [[nerve cell]] which conducts [[nerve impulse]]s from a [[synapse]] to the body of the cell; a [[dendron]].
#* '''1970''', {{w|Saul Bellow}}, ''{{w|Mr. Sammler's Planet|Mr. Sammler’s Planet}}'', Greenwich, CT: Fawcett, 1971, Chapter 1, pp.{{nbsp}}12-13,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/mrsammlersplanet1970bell]</sup>
#*: Little copses of television antennas. Whiplike, graceful thrilling metal '''dendrites''' drawing images from the air, bringing brotherhood, communion to immured apartment people.
#* '''1979''', {{w|Carl Sagan}}, “Can We Know the Universe? Reflections on a Grain of Salt” in John Carey (ed.), ''Eyewitness to Science'', Harvard University Press, 1997, p.{{nbsp}}437,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=Jzjp_sX0STgC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: A typical brain neuron has perhaps a thousand little wires, called '''dendrites''', which connect it with its fellows.
# {{lb|en|cytology}} Slender cell process emanating from the cell bodies of dendritic cells and follicular dendritic cells of the immune system.
# {{lb|en|crystallography|metallurgy}} Tree-like structure of crystals growing as material crystallizes
# A [[hermit]] who lived in a [[tree]]

